## 1. Title & Running Head

**Proposed Title:** *CAM-Sim: A Reproducible Benchmark for Situational-Awareness Ablations in Marketing Agents*  
**Running Head:** CAM-Sim Benchmark  
**Keywords:** Benchmark, Agentic AI, Situational Awareness, Marketing Automation, Ablation Study

---

## 2. Abstract (150-200 words)

**DRAFT:**
We present **CAM-Sim**, a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents: 50 seeds × 200 scenarios, eleven agents on a ladder from context-blind baseline through signal-based classifiers to a labeled oracle. Multi-signal situation classification raises match from 87.5% to 98.7% and profit from +$155 to +$205 (+$50, p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96). A matched-family ablation (same NCC family, 1 vs 3 signals) isolates the gain as signal-driven (+$103.85 (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 5.16e-46; +$103.10 (fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 1.60e-45). Standard classifier baselines (logistic regression, random forest) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the multi-signal advantage is classifier-family-robust (LR +$65.48, p = 2.2e-40; RF +$75.98, p = 3.2e-37; RF-3 achieves 100% match, slightly beating NCC). A context-blind policy search reveals a **channel-cost arbitrage**: EDUCATIONAL on EMAIL (cost $0.05) at bid 0.3 earns +$252, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments — the reward is channel-independent, so the cheapest channel dominates (§8.7). The advantage replicates across 9 environments (per-seed ρ ≥ 0.93), survives 30% label noise and budget pacing, and is checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000), macro-F1 (per-class), and common random numbers. We contribute a reproducible benchmark, environment-consistency evidence, and a pre-specified field-validation design. Eight v1.3 extensions (adversarial mapping, channel-dependent reward, bandit baselines, class-conditional noise, convergence trend, perception dose-response, crisis bid policy, sensing fidelity) close the remaining simulatable threats to validity. Three v1.4 extensions push further: online reward-feedback learners recover up to 83% of the fixed-mapping value from observed profit alone (§8.18); recalibrating to a mid-episode distribution shift is net-negative (−$15.08, p = 1.6e-05), refining the recalibration remedy (§8.19); and the flat taxonomy's predicted confusions are quantified (11.0% cross-axis confusion for intent-only vs 1.0% multi-signal, §8.20). A v1.5 extension executes the pre-registered LLM baseline: zero-shot classifiers (temperature 0, qualitative definitions only; five models from LAN-local to 128B hosted) reach 74–83% raw accuracy — profit-competitive with the hand-tuned classifier but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), losing to supervised classifiers, with emergent ≤4-of-6 blind spots (§8.21); the v1.6 extension shows the blindness is prior-driven (0/210 counterfactual probes ever elicit crisis/retention), prompt remediation restores recall only at a profit/accuracy cost (hints: Mistral +$7.49, glm -$57.75; few-shot: both -$69 to -$88), and end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds cam_multisignal without any fitted component (+$250–273 vs +$206–220).

---

## 3. Introduction

### 3.1 The Agentic Revolution in Marketing
The marketing corpus (snapshot 2026-11-02; over 15,700 papers) shows **at least 100 agentic papers** (all from 2025-2026; the count is a live lower bound — the corpus only grows) — the highest-momentum trend. Yet **zero papers** connect agentic capabilities with **marketing situational awareness** (at least 250 contextual papers, a small number of situational papers, and none combined with agentic).

**Research Gap:** Agents can plan and execute, but cannot **understand marketing context**.

### 3.2 The Häglund Prior Art Problem
Emil Häglund's 2025 thesis (*"Contextual intelligence: leveraging AI for targeted marketing"*, Umeå University, Dept. of Computing Science) stakes the phrase in **CS/NLP**. His work provides **technical foundations** (opinion-unit extraction, aspect-based sentiment, media-context effects) but **does not define a marketing construct**.

**Positioning:** While Häglund (2025) operationalizes contextual understanding in **text**, our work operationalizes **situational awareness** in **marketing agents**.

### 3.3 Research Questions
- **RQ1 (Benchmark):** What environment variations and agent designs does CAM-Sim discriminate, and does the discrimination replicate across 9 environments?
- **RQ2 (Ablation):** Which signal and bid components drive the profit differences — is the multi-signal gain signal-driven or classifier-capacity-driven? **Resolved (§8.8):** standard classifiers (LR, RF) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the gain is signal-driven across three classifier families (NCC +$103.85, LR +$65.48, RF +$75.98).
- **RQ3 (Performance):** Do context-aware agents outperform non-context-aware agents on marketing outcomes?
- **RQ4 (Mechanism):** Through which mechanisms does situational awareness improve marketing performance?

**Key Insight:** No paper in our corpus combines agentic reasoning with an explicit situational-awareness framework (agentic ∩ "situational awareness" = 0; agentic ∩ "situational" = 1 — Darwin-Claw, a lexical co-mention about agent self-evolution, not marketing situational awareness); agentic ∩ contextual has exactly 11 lexical co-mentions (snapshot 2026-11-02).

---

## 4. Theoretical Foundation

### 4.1 Situational Awareness Theory (Endsley, 1988/1995)
CAM is theoretically grounded in **Endsley's Three-Level Model** of Situational Awareness:

| SA Level | Definition | CAM Mapping |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| **Level 1: Perception** | Awareness of status, attributes, and dynamics of elements in environment | **Sensing Layer** — real-time ingestion of multi-modal signals |
| **Level 2: Comprehension** | Understanding of the current situation | **Context Model** — unified representation of audience, channel, temporal, situational, social, market context |
| **Level 3: Projection** | Ability to predict future states | **Awareness Engine** — Context Predictor for next-best context |

**Citation:** Endsley, M. R. (1988). Design and Evaluation for Situation Awareness Enhancement. Proceedings of the Human Factors Society Annual Meeting, 32(2), 97–101. Endsley, M. R. (1995). Toward a Theory of Situation Awareness in Dynamic Systems. Human Factors, 37(1), 32–64.

### 4.2 Agent Theory (Russell & Norvig, 2020)
Russell & Norvig define an **agent** as "anything that can be viewed as perceiving its environment through sensors and acting upon that environment through actuators."

**CAM as Rational Agent:**
- **Percepts:** Context signals (Sensing Layer)
- **Actions:** Marketing actions (Action Layer)
- **Performance Measure:** ROAS, Context Match Rate, Profit
- **Environment:** Marketing ecosystem (competitive, consumer, platform)

**Citation:** Russell, S. J., & Norvig, P. (2020). Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach (4th ed.).

### 4.3 Marketing Automation Context
**Foundational Work:**
- **Personalization:** Peppers & Rogers (1993) — The One-to-One Future
- **Contextual Targeting:** Godin (1999) — Permission Marketing
- **Autonomous Marketing:** Importance of autonomy in marketing decision-making (REFS from corpus)

**Gap:** None of these address **situational awareness** in autonomous agents.

---

## 5. Related Work

### 5.1 The Agentic Literature in Marketing (≥100 papers from corpus)
From our marketing-research corpus (over 15,700 papers; snapshot 2026-11-02; counts are live lower bounds — the corpus only grows):
- **Definition:** Works that mention "agentic" in title/abstract
- **Total:** ≥100 papers, all from 2025–2026 (a sustained, highest-momentum trend; the exact total grows with the corpus)
- **Categories:** concentrated in AI-Marketing (≥60) and Digital-Marketing (≥15), with smaller counts across Consumer-Behavior, Brand, Survey, and B2B

**Common Themes:**
- Agent-based simulation
- World models for marketing
- Autonomous decision systems

**Gap:** No paper systematically bridges agentic capabilities with contextual intelligence (11 lexical co-mentions as of 2026-11-02; §5.3). Zero papers combine agentic with situational awareness (exact phrase).

### 5.2 The Contextual Literature in Marketing (≥250 papers from corpus)
- **Total:** ≥250 papers mentioning "contextual" (live lower bound)
- **Recency:** concentrated in 2024–2026, with a strong 2026 uptick
- **Categories:** most common in AI-Marketing and Survey, followed by Consumer-Behavior, Digital-Marketing, Social-Media, and Brand; the remainder spread across CX-Retail, Privacy-Data, Content-Marketing, Analytics, and B2B

**Common Themes:**
- Contextual advertising (Kotler & Armstrong, 2021)
- Context-aware recommendations
- Contextual targeting in privacy-preserving ways

**Gap:** No address **agentic systems**. All contextual work assumes **human-driven** systems.

### 5.3 The Near-Zero-Intersection Problem
**Critical Finding (snapshot 2026-11-02):**
- **11 papers** in the corpus mention both "agentic" and "contextual" (all 2026); the closest is "Can AI Agents Simulate A/B Test Outcomes? A Validation Framework for Agentic Experimentation" (2026-08, AI-Marketing), and all are lexical co-mentions
- **1 paper** mentions both "agentic" and "situational" (Darwin-Claw, about agent self-evolution — not marketing situational awareness); **0 papers** mention both "agentic" and "situational awareness" (exact phrase) — the intersection relevant to this paper's construct
- **No paper** systematically bridges agentic reasoning with an explicit situational-awareness framework

**Scope caveat:** This is a keyword co-occurrence search (title/abstract) within our corpus of over 15,700 papers (snapshot 2026-11-02), not a systematic literature review, and the corpus is live and growing — counts drift between snapshots. Adjacent literatures — **contextual bandits** (Agrawal & Goyal, 2013; Li et al., 2010; production deployments at every major ad platform), **context-aware recommender systems** (Adomavicius & Tuzhilin, 2011), and **pervasive/ubiquitous computing** (Dey, 2001) — address context-aware action selection but do not use the "agentic + situational awareness" terminology. The near-zero-intersection finding is a terminological gap, not evidence of conceptual novelty; a systematic review is needed to substantiate a first-mover claim (§9.2 #13). A combined literature review (agentic + contextual intelligence, PRISMA-lite over the corpus, construct-level screening) is now in progress: `paper/lit_review_agentic_contextual.md` — its §5 refines the claim to a construct-level gap (two substantive candidates exist — a retail-pricing multi-agent framework and a contextualized-persuasion study — but neither defines a marketing construct); PRISMA flow: `paper/prisma_flow.svg`.

