401:**The remedy is remarkably label-robust up to ε = 0.3**: intent-only match rates degrade by ≤ 0.6 pp (crisis 75.7→75.2, retention 84.7→84.9) and profits are broadly stable — the greedy interval fit on majority labels survives sparse corruption. The multi-signal recalibrated classifier degrades more in match rate (crisis 98.9→90.0, retention 98.9→84.9) but stays strongly profitable everywhere (≥ +$106.3 at ε = 0.3). Under `uniform_situations`, intent-only recalibration goes negative at ε ≥ 0.2 (−$26.1), while multi-signal recalibration stays at +$133.7 — the F6 error-placement effect amplified by label noise. The F5→F6 story does not depend on an idealized labeling process; multi-signal inference is the more robust remedy.
494:Macro-F1 confirms the multi-signal advantage at the per-class level: 3-signal classifiers (0.92–1.00) substantially outperform 1-signal classifiers (0.46–0.73). `cam_inferred`'s low macro-F1 (0.4696) reflects its structural handicap (4-of-6 classes), which match rate (75.7%) overstates.
546:**Findings.** (1) **Reward feedback alone recovers most of the mapping value:** UCB1 closes 83.0% of the baseline→`cam_multisignal` profit gap in the default environment (81.3% in crisis_heavy); ε-greedy closes 74.6% / 72.5%. Both remain significantly below the fixed mapping (UCB1: −$64.00, p = 2.8e-59, d = −4.13; ε-greedy: −$95.37, p = 3.7e-50, d = −5.76). (2) **The label-free online learner beats the hand-tuned classifier:** `online_ucb` (+$142.13) outperforms `cam_inferred` (+$104.26) without a single labeled action example (paired +$37.87, p = 4.8e-25, d = 2.37; ε-greedy also wins: +$6.50, p = 4.7e-03), though it stays significantly below `cam_learned` (+$155.38, 2,000 labeled samples; paired −$13.25, p = 7.6e-10). (The online runner reproduces the core benchmark byte-identically for the shared deterministic agents — baseline −$170.03, `cam_inferred` +$104.26, `cam_multisignal` +$206.13 — validating pairing fidelity.) (3) **The bottleneck is rare situations:** crisis and opportunity have 5% base rates (≈10 episode visits each), so their Q-cells stay under-sampled within a 200-step window — the learned mapping converges on high-frequency situations first. (4) **Scope:** channel and bid rules are given (only the action mapping is learned); joint (action × channel × bid) learning is confounded by the §8.7 channel-cost arbitrage and left open, as is online adaptation of the bid layer.
548:**F10 (online recovery bound):** the mapping's value is *partially* recoverable from pure reward feedback (72.5–83.0% of the fixed-mapping gap), but labeled situation data remains the efficient path to full value — supervised classifiers trained on 2,000 labels dominate online learners at 200 steps.
578:### 8.21 LLM Zero-Shot Classification Baseline (v1.5)
580:The pre-registered protocol of §10.3 #24, executed on 2026-11-02 (`paper/cam_sim_v15_llm.py`; results `results/cam_sim_v15.json`, response cache `results/llm_cache/`). Each context is textualized into a natural-language observation — intent strength, competitive density, channel quality, and the verbatim signals list *including the decoy `quality_score`* — and sent to an LLM at temperature 0 whose system prompt states the six situations as **qualitative marketing definitions only** (no numeric centroids, no examples; the full prompt is in the repo for audit). The returned label drives the same fixed pipeline as every CAM agent: `IDEAL_ACTION` action type, `SITUATION_CHANNEL` channel, oracle-style bid. The agent is therefore *identical to `cam_multisignal` except for the classifier* — any profit difference is attributable to classification quality alone. Evaluation runs on the identical per-seed context sequences (pairing-guard asserted against `run_online_env` byte-identically).
582:**Protocol deviation (documented):** the pre-registered 50-seed protocol (10,000 calls per model) exceeded measured provider quotas (SAIA: 800 requests/day; Groq: daily token caps; measured 2026-11-02), so per-model seed counts are reduced and reported exactly: `zai/glm-4.7` 10 seeds (2,000 calls), `saia/qwen3.6-35b-a3b` 2 seeds (400 calls), `groq/qwen3.8-27b` 3 of 5 planned seeds complete (600 calls; provider token quota), `tud/mistral-medium-3.5-128b` 10 seeds (2,000 calls; TU Darmstadt llm-service), `local/deepseek-v4-flash` 5 seeds (1,000 calls; LAN-local reasoning model — the only quota-free deployment, ~9 s/call). All comparisons remain paired on identical sequences; scenario-level contrasts (n = seeds × 200) are primary, seed-level t-tests secondary (low power at n < 10).
