26:> **Status:** DRAFT (v1.6); snapshot 2026-11-03 — results auto-generated from the CAM-Sim benchmark (v0.4 core + v1.3/v1.4/v1.5/v1.6 extensions); peer-reviewed (5 rounds: stats-audit, citation-audit, threats-to-validity, 5× journal-editor, 5× adversarial-reviewer, 5× domain-expert, 5× ml-methodology); all numbers regenerate from cam_sim.py, cam_sim_ablations.py, cam_sim_extensions.py, cam_sim_v14.py, cam_sim_v15_llm.py, cam_sim_v16_arms.py, and results/cam_sim_v16.md  
30:> **Key Contribution:** A reproducible paired-seed benchmark (CAM-Sim) for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents: 11-agent ladder, 9 environments, matched-family ablation (same NCC classifier, 1 vs 3 signals, +$103.85 signal-driven gap), standard classifier baselines (LR + RF on 1 and 3 signals — multi-signal gain confirmed across 3 classifier families: NCC +$103.85, LR +$65.48, RF +$75.98), context-blind policy search (channel-cost arbitrage: EDUCATIONAL|EMAIL|bid 0.3 = +$252 beats every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments — §8.7), macro-F1 per-class metric, CRN check, paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000); v1.3: adversarial-mapping condition (0.4% match, -$269), channel-dependent reward (arbitrage fixed), bandit baselines (epsilon-greedy, LinUCB), class-conditional noise, convergence trend (Spearman + Geweke), perception dose-response (rho=0.998), crisis bid policy (pause > counter), sensing fidelity (identical); v1.4: online reward-feedback learning (UCB1 recovers 83.0% of the fixed-mapping value with zero labeled action data), mid-episode distribution drift (post-drift recalibration is net-negative; multi-signal inference is drift-robust), flat-taxonomy confusion quantification (intent-only 11.0% cross-axis confusion vs 1.0% multi-signal); v1.5: LLM zero-shot classification baseline (§8.21) — five models across five providers (incl. a university-hosted 128B Mistral and a LAN-local reasoning model) at temperature 0 reach 74–83% accuracy at $0 API cost, profit-competitive with the hand-tuned single-signal classifier but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), all losing to 2,000-label supervised classifiers, with emergent ≤4-of-6 blind spots (never predicting crisis/retention) mirroring the hand-tuned classifier's structural handicap  
46:We present **CAM-Sim**, a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents: 50 seeds × 200 scenarios, eleven agents on a ladder from context-blind baseline through signal-based classifiers to a labeled oracle. Multi-signal situation classification raises match from 87.5% to 98.7% and profit from +$155 to +$205 (+$50, p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96). A matched-family ablation (same NCC family, 1 vs 3 signals) isolates the gain as signal-driven (+$103.85 (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 5.16e-46; +$103.10 (fitted 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal), p = 1.60e-45). Standard classifier baselines (logistic regression, random forest) on 1 and 3 signals confirm the multi-signal advantage is classifier-family-robust (LR +$65.48, p = 2.2e-40; RF +$75.98, p = 3.2e-37; RF-3 achieves 100% match, slightly beating NCC). A context-blind policy search reveals a **channel-cost arbitrage**: EDUCATIONAL on EMAIL (cost $0.05) at bid 0.3 earns +$252, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 of 9 environments — the reward is channel-independent, so the cheapest channel dominates (§8.7). The advantage replicates across 9 environments (per-seed ρ ≥ 0.93), survives 30% label noise and budget pacing, and is checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000), macro-F1 (per-class), and common random numbers. We contribute a reproducible benchmark, environment-consistency evidence, and a pre-specified field-validation design. Eight v1.3 extensions (adversarial mapping, channel-dependent reward, bandit baselines, class-conditional noise, convergence trend, perception dose-response, crisis bid policy, sensing fidelity) close the remaining simulatable threats to validity. Three v1.4 extensions push further: online reward-feedback learners recover up to 83% of the fixed-mapping value from observed profit alone (§8.18); recalibrating to a mid-episode distribution shift is net-negative (−$15.08, p = 1.6e-05), refining the recalibration remedy (§8.19); and the flat taxonomy's predicted confusions are quantified (11.0% cross-axis confusion for intent-only vs 1.0% multi-signal, §8.20). A v1.5 extension executes the pre-registered LLM baseline: zero-shot classifiers (temperature 0, qualitative definitions only; five models from LAN-local to 128B hosted) reach 74–83% raw accuracy — profit-competitive with the hand-tuned classifier but below it on balanced accuracy (macro-recall 50–61% vs 65.9%), losing to supervised classifiers, with emergent ≤4-of-6 blind spots (§8.21); the v1.6 extension shows the blindness is prior-driven (0/210 counterfactual probes ever elicit crisis/retention), prompt remediation restores recall only at a profit/accuracy cost (hints: Mistral +$7.49, glm -$57.75; few-shot: both -$69 to -$88), and end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds cam_multisignal without any fitted component (+$250–273 vs +$206–220).
147:| **Construct** | — | Context vector → arm selection | **Contextual Intelligence in Marketing (CIM)** |
153:**Positioning vs contextual bandits:** Contextual bandits (Li et al., 2010; Agrawal & Goyal, 2013) are the closest algorithmic neighbor — they select actions from context signals and are deployed at scale in ad platforms. CAM differs in three respects: (1) bandits optimize a scalar reward from a fixed arm set; CAM introduces a **situation taxonomy** (6 archetypes) that mediates context→action mapping; (2) bandits learn online from observed rewards; CAM-Sim evaluates offline classification quality against ground-truth labels; (3) bandits do not model **situational awareness** (Endsley Levels 1–3) — they are context-aware but not situation-aware. A systematic comparison with contextual-bandit baselines is deferred to future work (§10.3).
250:- **H4 (environment-consistency):** Performance will increase with situational-awareness quality — tested both by label-ordering under default economics and label-free (Spearman ρ of match rate vs. profit across agents) across 9 environment presets (Section 8.4)
285:- **Comparison:** every agent vs. baseline (seed-level paired t-tests); H4 tested both by label-ordering (§8.3 F2) and label-free as the per-seed distribution of Spearman ρ(match rate, profit) across agents (§8.4)
293:- **Multiplicity:** 10 agents × 6 metrics vs baseline per environment (60 tests); the smallest p-value is 3.3e-70 and the largest is 8.5e-15, both far below a Bonferroni-corrected α = 0.05/60 = 8.3e-4, so all reported comparisons survive familywise correction within a single environment. Across 9 environments plus exploratory grids (α-sweep, label-noise, budget-pacing), the full family is 540 confirmatory tests (10 agents × 6 metrics × 9 environments) + 3 ablation contrasts + 5 context-blind policies + 5 standard-baseline contrasts (LR/RF, §8.8) + 12 v1.3 extension contrasts (§8.10–§8.17) + 5 v1.4 extension contrasts (§8.18–§8.20) + 40 v1.5 LLM-baseline contrasts (8 per model × 5 models: 4 seed-level + 4 scenario-level, §8.21) + 20 v1.6 prompt-variant contrasts (arms E/F/H profit means and model-vs-reference contrasts, section 8.22) = 630 total; all confirmatory p-values remain far below any reasonable correction; the §9.2.1 near-equivalence caveat (p = 2.5e-04) does not survive Bonferroni at α/565, which itself confirms the agents are near-identical.
370:**F7 (label-free environment-consistency check, with proper inference):** Label-ordered ladders can mislead (an 87.5%-accurate classifier *should* exceed an 80%-perception agent). The label-free test — Spearman ρ(context match rate, profit) across agents — is computed **within each seed** (50 paired replicates of an 11-agent ranking (plus 2 recalibration variants in the label-noise grid, for 13 total)) and reported as mean [95% CI]: **0.94–0.99 across all nine environments, with every lower CI bound ≥ 0.93** (Table). Monotonicity of profit in actual perception quality is thus established with a paired design rather than a pseudo-inferential p-value over non-independent agents. The moderator from F6 remains: error *placement* can suppress profit below what match rate alone predicts.
426:**Matched-family ablation.** A nearest-centroid classifier on intent-only (`ncc_intent_only`, same NCC family as `cam_multisignal`, 1 signal instead of 3) achieves 72.7% match and +$102.28 profit. The 3-signal NCC (`cam_multisignal`, 98.9% match, +$206.13) outperforms it by +$103.85 (p = 5.16e-46, paired-diff bootstrap CI [+$100.13, +$107.55]). **Disclosure:** `cam_multisignal` uses hand-set generative centroids (the true means), while `ncc_intent_only` uses centroids fitted from a 2,000-sample calibration (seed 999999); the 3-signal arm is therefore an upper bound on the fitted NCC estimator, not a lower bound. The learned-vs-learned comparison (`cam_multisignal_learned` vs `cam_learned`, both fitted) gives +$50.01 (p = 8.5e-32, d_z = 3.96) — the conservative learned-vs-learned estimate. The +$103.85 gap is the family-matched maximum (hand-set 3-signal vs fitted 1-signal); the learned-vs-learned gap (+$50.01) is the conservative estimate. Both are signal-driven, not classifier-capacity-driven (same NCC family, same bid policy, same action mapping; differs in signal count and fitting method).
516:We test for slow monotone drift (Section 9.2 #17) with Spearman rho of profit vs seed index (1..50) and a Geweke z-score (first 20% vs last 50%). cam_multisignal: rho = -0.301 (p = 0.034, marginally significant), Geweke z = 2.04 (borderline). baseline: rho = 0.189 (p = 0.188), z = -1.05. cam_learned: rho = -0.176 (p = 0.221), z = 0.39. The marginal cam_multisignal trend (rho = -0.301) reflects slightly higher profits in early seeds, not a systematic drift; the Geweke z = 2.04 is borderline (|z| < 1.96 would be non-significant). The paired t-tests (which use per-seed differencing) are robust to this trend. Conclusion: no strong convergence concern, but the marginal trend is reported for transparency.
520:To de-confound perception from bidding (Section 9.2 #4, Section 10.3 #4), we run NoisyCAM with flat bidding (bid = 1.0, no situation-based multiplier) at p_correct in {0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.0}. Results: profit increases monotonically from +$128.79 (p=0.5, 58.1% match) to +$294.27 (p=1.0, 100% match). Per-seed Spearman rho(profit, p) = 0.998 (min 0.943). This confirms the perception->profit dose-response is not an artifact of bid policy: even with flat bidding, better perception yields higher profit. The p=1.0 profit (+$294.27) equals situation_only (+$294.27), confirming that flat bidding is optimal when perception is perfect; the oracle's 2.0x crisis multiplier is suboptimal (Section 8.16).
582:**Protocol deviation (documented):** the pre-registered 50-seed protocol (10,000 calls per model) exceeded measured provider quotas (SAIA: 800 requests/day; Groq: daily token caps; measured 2026-11-02), so per-model seed counts are reduced and reported exactly: `zai/glm-4.7` 10 seeds (2,000 calls), `saia/qwen3.6-35b-a3b` 2 seeds (400 calls), `groq/qwen3.8-27b` 3 of 5 planned seeds complete (600 calls; provider token quota), `tud/mistral-medium-3.5-128b` 10 seeds (2,000 calls; TU Darmstadt llm-service), `local/deepseek-v4-flash` 5 seeds (1,000 calls; LAN-local reasoning model — the only quota-free deployment, ~9 s/call). All comparisons remain paired on identical sequences; scenario-level contrasts (n = seeds × 200) are primary, seed-level t-tests secondary (low power at n < 10).
597:**F11 (LLM zero-shot ceiling):** LLMs at temperature 0 — from a LAN-local reasoning model to a 128B dense model — classify CAM-Sim contexts at 74–83% raw accuracy, but that accuracy is prior-inflated: **macro-recall is only 50–61%** (below the hand-tuned classifier's 65.9%) with zero recall on crisis and retention; skill concentrates in the funnel trio (big-3 recall 87–100%), which is where 80.3% of contexts live. qwen3.6-35b's nominal +$0.10 vs `cam_inferred` (p = 8.1e-03; does not survive 610-way Bonferroni correction) is not a reliable win — the surviving contrasts are glm-4.7 (+$0.07, p = 8.9e-06) and Mistral-128B (+$0.09, p = 1.2e-08), with DeepSeek significantly negative (−$0.10, p = 4.9e-06). LLM situation classification is thus at best a *label-free starting point* — profitable at the margin but balanced-classification-inferior — while supervised multi-signal classifiers remain the efficient path to full value in this environment. The v1.6 arms LOCALIZE the zero-recall blindness as **prior-driven** (0/210 counterfactual probes ever elicit crisis/retention) and show that prompt remediation restores recall only at a profit/accuracy cost (hints: Mistral +$7.49, glm -$57.75; few-shot: both -$69 to -$88), while end-to-end action selection (v1.6 arm H) exceeds cam_multisignal in both families (+$273.64 / +$250.28).
599:**Scope:** classification-only per the pre-registration (the action/channel/bid mapping is given, isolating classifier quality); prompt-level remediation (few-shot, qualitative hints, self-consistency) and end-to-end LLM action selection are now tested empirically in v1.6 (section 8.22); fine-tuned LLM classifiers remain future work. Responses are cached (one snapshot; providers do not guarantee bitwise determinism at temperature 0 — exact regeneration requires fresh API calls). Provider rate limits (documented above) make this the benchmark's first experiment whose sample size is externally constrained; the protocol scales to the full 50 seeds as quotas allow.
601:### 8.22 Prompt-Variant Diagnostics, Remediation, and End-to-End Agency (v1.6)
603:This v1.6 extension localizes the 8.21 blindness cause and tests remediation vs end-to-end agency. It is a strict pre-registration addition (item 24 follow-up), all numbers from `results/cam_sim_v16.md`, no fitted components, seed-level bootstrap CIs (10k reps, RNG 20261103). Eight arms: A order-permutation, B minimal-labels, C counterfactual-archetype probes, D decoy-removal, E qualitative-hints, F few-shot, G self-consistency, H end-to-end action selection. Arms A, B, C, D, G are descriptive diagnostics (no confirmatory claim); arms E, F, H carry inferential contrasts (profit means and model-vs-reference CIs).
611:**(C) End-to-end (H) headline.** Arm H (end-to-end, seeds 1-5; LLM outputs the full decision line 'situation; action; channel; bid' from menus: six situations × six actions × five channels × five bid tiers {0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0, 1.4}, plus six normative sentences; evaluated directly via evaluate_action on the canonical per-seed env; invalid/failed → fixed neutral fallback Action(educational, search, 1.0) counted as invalid): Mistral end-to-end: profit +$273.64 [260.67, 286.61], ideal-action match 81.6%, invalid 0/1000; vs cam_inferred +$158.53 [142.71, 174.76], vs cam_multisignal +$53.35 [39.63, 67.21], vs oracle +$49.15 [35.19, 63.76]. glm end-to-end: profit +$250.28 [240.90, 259.57], match 74.7%, invalid 13/1000; vs cam_inferred +$135.18, vs cam_multisignal +$29.99 [19.31, 40.94], vs oracle +$25.79 [14.65, 37.38]. **Headline:** end-to-end semantic agency beats the ENTIRE fixed-mapper stack in both families, including cam_multisignal (supervised on 2,000 labels) and the situation-only oracle row (fixed bids) — with NO fitted component. The LLM natively chooses pause/loyalty/urgent actions for off-funnel situations, reproducing the crisis-policy insight (§8.16: pause beats counter) from normative text alone. Proper wall-clock ceiling remains bid_calibrated +$530.45. First agent class to exceed the fixed-mapper ceiling in either family without any fitted component.
624:- **H4 (Environment-consistency):** ✅ Supported — per-seed Spearman ρ(match rate, profit) 0.94–0.99 across all 9 environments, every 95% CI lower bound 
