436:\finding{Systematic bias beats unbiased noise, adversely (F5): The label-ordered H4 ladder holds under default and economic shifts, but breaks in all four distribution-shifted presets. \Ag{am_inferred} falls below even unbiased 50\% perception under crisis\_heavy (-\$66.8 vs noisy50 -\$1.9) and retention\_heavy (-\$15.5 vs +\$60.6). Mechanism: the classifier's errors are systematic, so bias concentrates where probability mass is, while noisy agents' unbiased errors average out. The multi-signal classifier eliminates this failure (F9): \Ag{am_multisignal} stays strongly positive in all four shifted presets (+\$160.6, +\$65.3, +\$141.9, +\$237.2).}
438:\finding{Recalibration is the remedy -- and multi-signal is the better remedy (F6): Refitting the same learner per distribution recovers most of the F5 loss (crisis\_heavy -\$66.8 $\rightarrow$ +\$27.1; retention\_heavy -\$15.5 $\rightarrow$ +\$164.5). Multi-signal recalibration tracks the hand-set multi-signal agent closely and dominates intent-only recalibration. Exception: under uniform\_situations intent-only recalibration earns less than noisy50 under label noise (-\$26.1 vs +\$7.9) despite a higher match rate (67.0\%) --- error placement, not quantity, drives the profit relationship. Match rate is not profit.}
