741:\textbf{Headline:} end-to-end semantic agency beats the entire fixed-mapper stack in both families, including \Ag{am_multisignal} (supervised on 2,000 labels) and the situation-only oracle row (fixed bids) --- with no fitted component. The LLM natively chooses pause/loyalty/urgent actions for off-funnel situations, reproducing the crisis-policy insight (\S8.16: pause beats counter) from normative text alone. The proper wall-clock ceiling remains \Ag{id_calibrated} (+\$530.45). This is the first agent class to exceed the fixed-mapper ceiling in either family without any fitted component.
751:\textbf{H1 (Context match):} Supported --- every situation-aware agent reaches 59.6--100\% match vs 21.6\% baseline.\\
752:\textbf{H2 (Profit):} Supported --- all context-aware agents beat baseline (channel-only improves to -\$110.16 but stays unprofitable).\\
753:\textbf{H3 (ROAS):} Supported --- aggregate ROAS rises from 0.484 (baseline) to 1.20--2.61 for heuristic agents and 26.37 for \Ag{id_calibrated}.\\
758:\textbf{Honest framing of the oracle:} The oracle is an upper bound validating environment consistency. The scientifically meaningful agents are \Ag{am_multisignal} (98.9\%, within \$4 of oracle but the \$3.87 gap is significant, p = 1.3e-13), \Ag{am_multisignal_learned} (98.7\%), \Ag{am_inferred} (single-signal, 75.7\%, 4-of-6), and the noisy agents. The hand-set \Ag{am_multisignal} uses true generative centroids; 98.9\% is the nearest-centroid lower bound, not the Bayes rate (prior-weighted MAP achieves $\approx$99.2\%). The fair single-signal comparator is \Ag{am_learned} (87.5\%, +\$155.38), not the handicapped \Ag{am_inferred}: learned-vs-learned gain +\$50.01, handicapped +\$101.87.
772:\item \textbf{Seed pairing is fair but low-power.} Seed-level correlation between baseline and agents is near zero ($-$0.23 to +0.08); pairing buys no power advantage. \resolved{8.9}{CRN changes profits by $<$\$3; conclusions unchanged.}
