792:\textbf{Multiplicity:} All p-values survive Bonferroni (630 total family; \S7.3); \S8.21 rows keep the historical 610-way annotation. \textbf{Normality:} profit distributions are unimodal and approximately symmetric at seed level; paired t-test robust at n = 50. \textbf{Equivalence testing:} ``Within \$4 of the oracle'' is descriptive --- the \$3.87 gap is significant (p = 1.3e-13). ``Essentially matches'' claims should carry the caveat that differences are statistically detectable at n = 50 even when economically negligible.
796:\textbf{Bootstrap BCa CIs (200 resamples):} check normal-approx CIs within \$1.4 for all 11 agents (max noisy80 \$1.31); $|z_0| \le 0.16$, $|acceleration| \le 0.014$. Caveat: B = 200 has Monte-Carlo endpoint noise of $\approx$\$2--3.5; paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000, Table~\ref{ab:bootstrap}) confirms within \$0.30.
825:We presented CAM-Sim, a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents. Multi-signal classification raises match from 87.5\% to 98.7\% and profit +\$155$\rightarrow$+\$205 (+\$50, \pv{8.5e-32}); a matched-family ablation isolates the gain as signal-driven. A context-blind policy search reveals a channel-cost arbitrage (EDUCATIONAL/EMAIL/0.3 earns +\$252). The advantage replicates across 9 environments, survives 30\% label noise and pacing, checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000), macro-F1, and CRN. v1.4 adds online reward-feedback learning (83\% gap recovery), drift robustness, and taxonomy-confusion quantification. v1.5 executes the pre-registered zero-shot LLM baseline (74--83\% accuracy, 4-of-6 blind spots). v1.6 localizes blindness as prior-driven and shows end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds \Ag{am_multisignal} without any fitted component (+\$250--273 vs +\$206--220). All profits are scaled-reward proxies. The practical takeaway: invest first in multi-signal situation classification, then in mechanism-calibrated bidding --- never in unvalidated bid heuristics.
