64:  We present \textbf{CAM-Sim}, a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents: 50 seeds $\times$ 200 scenarios, eleven agents on a ladder from context-blind baseline through signal-based classifiers to a labeled oracle. Multi-signal situation classification raises match from 87.5\% to 98.7\% and profit from +\$155 to +\$205 (+\$50, \pv{8.5\!\times\!10^{-32}}, $d_z\!=\!3.96$). A matched-family ablation isolates the gain as signal-driven (+\$103.85, \pv{5.16\!\times\!10^{-46}}; +\$103.10 learned-vs-learned, \pv{1.60\!\times\!10^{-45}}). Standard baselines confirm the advantage across three classifier families (LR +\$65.48, \pvl{10^{-40}}; RF +\$75.98, \pvl{10^{-37}}). A context-blind policy search reveals a channel-cost arbitrage: EDUCATIONAL on EMAIL at bid 0.3 earns +\$252, beating every deployable context-aware agent in 7 environments. The advantage replicates across 9 environments (per-seed \srho $\ge0.93$), survives 30\% label noise and budget pacing, checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B $=\!2000$), macro-F1, and common random numbers. v1.5 executes the pre-registered zero-shot LLM baseline (74--83\% accuracy, macro-recall 50--61\% vs. 65.9\%, 4-of-6 blind spots). v1.6 shows blindness is prior-driven, prompt remediation is a trade-off, and end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds \Ag{am_multisignal} with no fitted component (+\$250--273 vs. +\$206--220).
409:Nine presets vary the environment economics while holding the situation-to-action language fixed: distribution shifts (uniform, decision-, crisis-, retention-heavy), doubled costs, weakened context payoffs, budget cap (\$250/episode), and concave returns (reward x (bid/clearing price)$^{0.5}$, capped at 2x). \Ag{am_learned} is fit once on default samples; \Ag{am_recalibrated} is refit per environment.
436:\finding{Systematic bias beats unbiased noise, adversely (F5): The label-ordered H4 ladder holds under default and economic shifts, but breaks in all four distribution-shifted presets. \Ag{am_inferred} falls below even unbiased 50\% perception under crisis\_heavy (-\$66.8 vs noisy50 -\$1.9) and retention\_heavy (-\$15.5 vs +\$60.6). Mechanism: the classifier's errors are systematic, so bias concentrates where probability mass is, while noisy agents' unbiased errors average out. The multi-signal classifier eliminates this failure (F9): \Ag{am_multisignal} stays strongly positive in all four shifted presets (+\$160.6, +\$65.3, +\$141.9, +\$237.2).}
504:\textbf{Matched-family ablation.} Nearest-centroid on intent-only (\Ag{cc_intent_only}, 1 signal, fitted) achieves 72.7\% match and +\$102.28. The 3-signal NCC (\Ag{am_multisignal}, 98.9\%, +\$206.13, hand-set generative centroids) outperforms by +\$103.85 (\pv{5.16\!\times\!10^{-46}}, paired bootstrap CI +\$100.13..+\$107.55). The learned-vs-learned comparison gives +\$50.01 (\pv{8.5\!\times\!10^{-32}}, d$_z$=3.96). Both gaps are signal-driven, not classifier-capacity-driven (same NCC family).
542:\Ag{am_multisignal} (ref.) & --- & --- & --- & 98.9\% & +\$206.13 \\ \bottomrule
578:\textbf{Macro-F1} averages per-class F1, penalizing classifiers that never predict minority classes (seed 1, 200 scenarios): \Ag{f_multisignal} 1.000, \Ag{r_multisignal} 0.931, \Ag{am_multisignal} 0.925, \Ag{am_multisignal_learned} 0.919, \Ag{f_intent_only} 0.728, \Ag{am_learned} 0.706, \Ag{cc_intent_only} 0.534, \Ag{am_inferred} 0.470 (emits only 4 of 6 classes), \Ag{r_intent_only} 0.463. The 3-signal classifiers (0.92--1.00) substantially outperform 1-signal (0.46--0.73).
584:The channel-cost arbitrage arises because base rewards are indexed by (action, situation), not channel. Adding a channel-mismatch penalty (0.5) drops the arbitrage policy \Ag{b_edu_email_030} from +\$252.41 to +\$132.72 (-47.5\%) while \Ag{am_multisignal} is nearly unchanged (+\$206.13 $\rightarrow$ +\$205.77). The arbitrage disappears: \Ag{am_multisignal} beats it by +\$73.05. This confirms the arbitrage is a reward-design artifact, not a feature of context-aware decisioning.
588:An agent with perfect 3-signal classification (98.9\% match) but a deranged action mapping (EXPLORATION$\rightarrow$PROMOTIONAL, \ldots, RETENTION$\rightarrow$URGENT) achieves 0.4\% match against the true ideal action and profit -\$268.88 (worse than the random baseline, -\$170.03). Contrast vs \Ag{am_multisignal}: +\$475.01 (\pv{5.23\!\times\!10^{-96}}, d = 29.28). Perfect classification with a wrong mapping is catastrophic; the benchmark is falsifiable via profit.
592:Two offline-trained bandits: (a) $\varepsilon$-greedy (3-signal NCC classification, $\varepsilon{=}0.1$ random action, else Q-argmax) earns +\$169.91 (90.1\% match; -\$36.22 vs \Ag{am_multisignal}, \pv{2.95\!\times\!10^{-31}}); (b) LinUCB-style linear profit model (ridge per action type) earns +\$91.42 (75.6\% match; -\$114.71, \pv{4.13\!\times\!10^{-48}}). Both underperform \Ag{am_multisignal} (+\$206.13). The linear model's failure confirms the reward landscape is nonlinear.
595:Class-conditional noise (crisis$\leftrightarrow$decision, retention$\leftrightarrow$exploration; consideration/opportunity clean) at 10/20/30\% drops \Ag{am_multisignal_learned} match from 98.7\% to 96.3/93.0/89.2\% and profit from +\$205.39 to +\$198.20/+\$186.59/+\$172.27. The 30\% contrast is -\$33.11 (\pv{1.13\!\times\!10^{-34}}). Class-conditional noise is less damaging than uniform noise at the same rate (only 4 of 6 classes corrupted).
598:Spearman $\rho$ of profit vs seed index (1..50) and Geweke z (first 20\% vs last 50\%): \Ag{am_multisignal} $\rho = -0.301$ (p = 0.034, marginal), z = 2.04 (borderline); baseline $\rho = 0.189$ (p = 0.188), z = -1.05; \Ag{am_learned} $\rho = -0.176$ (p = 0.221), z = 0.39. The marginal trend reflects higher profits in early seeds, not systematic drift; paired t-tests (per-seed differencing) are robust to it.
611:The bandit baselines were \textit{offline-trained} (no reward-feedback loop). Two online agents mirror \Ag{am_multisignal} (same 3-signal classifier, same channel rule, same heuristic bid) and differ only in action selection, learned from observed profit within a single 200-step episode:
631:\item Reward feedback alone recovers most of the mapping value (F10): UCB1 closes 83.0\% of the gap (default) / 81.3\% (crisis); $\varepsilon$-greedy 74.6\%/72.5\%. Online learners beat the hand-tuned \Ag{am_inferred} (+\$142.13 vs +\$104.26, paired +\$37.87, \pv{4.8e-25}) with no labeled examples, but stay below the 2,000-label supervised \Ag{am_learned} (-\$13.25, \pv{7.6e-10}). Bottleneck: rare situations (5\% base rate, $\approx$10 visits) keep Q-cells under-sampled.
673:Pre-registered protocol of \S10.3 \#24, executed 2026-11-02 (\Ag{am_sim_v15_llm.py}). Each context is textualized (intent strength, competitive density, channel quality, and the verbatim signals list incl.\ the \emph{decoy} quality\_score) and sent to an LLM at temperature 0 whose system prompt states the six situations as qualitative marketing definitions only (no numeric centroids, no examples). The returned label drives the same fixed pipeline as every CAM agent; the LLM agent is identical to \Ag{am_multisignal} except for the classifier.
741:\textbf{Headline:} end-to-end semantic agency beats the entire fixed-mapper stack in both families, including \Ag{am_multisignal} (supervised on 2,000 labels) and the situation-only oracle row (fixed bids) --- with no fitted component. The LLM natively chooses pause/loyalty/urgent actions for off-funnel situations, reproducing the crisis-policy insight (\S8.16: pause beats counter) from normative text alone. The proper wall-clock ceiling remains \Ag{id_calibrated} (+\$530.45). This is the first agent class to exceed the fixed-mapper ceiling in either family without any fitted component.
758:\textbf{Honest framing of the oracle:} The oracle is an upper bound validating environment consistency. The scientifically meaningful agents are \Ag{am_multisignal} (98.9\%, within \$4 of oracle but the \$3.87 gap is significant, p = 1.3e-13), \Ag{am_multisignal_learned} (98.7\%), \Ag{am_inferred} (single-signal, 75.7\%, 4-of-6), and the noisy agents. The hand-set \Ag{am_multisignal} uses true generative centroids; 98.9\% is the nearest-centroid lower bound, not the Bayes rate (prior-weighted MAP achieves $\approx$99.2\%). The fair single-signal comparator is \Ag{am_learned} (87.5\%, +\$155.38), not the handicapped \Ag{am_inferred}: learned-vs-learned gain +\$50.01, handicapped +\$101.87.
767:\item \textbf{\Ag{am_inferred} is a structurally handicapped comparator} (4-of-6 classes); \Ag{am_learned} (87.5\%, emit-any-class) is the fairer intent-only comparator.
798:\textbf{Seed-count convergence:} \Ag{am_multisignal} stays within 5\% of the 50-seed value from 20 seeds onward (+\$208.52 at 20 $\rightarrow$ +\$206.13 at 50, 1.2\% drift); per-seed $\rho$ stable at 0.981. Baseline shows largest drift (4.2\%). Conclusion: default 50-seed design adequate; 20 seeds suffice; convergence not formally established.
825:We presented CAM-Sim, a reproducible paired-seed benchmark for situational-awareness ablations in marketing agents. Multi-signal classification raises match from 87.5\% to 98.7\% and profit +\$155$\rightarrow$+\$205 (+\$50, \pv{8.5e-32}); a matched-family ablation isolates the gain as signal-driven. A context-blind policy search reveals a channel-cost arbitrage (EDUCATIONAL/EMAIL/0.3 earns +\$252). The advantage replicates across 9 environments, survives 30\% label noise and pacing, checked by paired-difference bootstrap (B = 2000), macro-F1, and CRN. v1.4 adds online reward-feedback learning (83\% gap recovery), drift robustness, and taxonomy-confusion quantification. v1.5 executes the pre-registered zero-shot LLM baseline (74--83\% accuracy, 4-of-6 blind spots). v1.6 localizes blindness as prior-driven and shows end-to-end LLM action selection exceeds \Ag{am_multisignal} without any fitted component (+\$250--273 vs +\$206--220). All profits are scaled-reward proxies. The practical takeaway: invest first in multi-signal situation classification, then in mechanism-calibrated bidding --- never in unvalidated bid heuristics.
921:\item \textbf{Calibration:} \Ag{am_learned}/\Ag{am_recalibrated} fit on 2,000 labeled samples (env seed 999999), interval classifier via greedy error-minimizing splits; \Ag{am_multisignal*} use nearest-centroid on 3 signals; label-noise stress at $\varepsilon \in \{0,.05,.1,.2,.3\}$.

